SP  - 206
EP  - 214
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3772128.3772162
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3772128.3772162
AB  - With the digitalization of document images accelerates, there is a growing demand for processing relevant contents within these images. The diverse nature of document images, along with substantial variations in stamp position and color, poses a challenge for existing methods to accurately extract stamps. The inability of most existing methods to simultaneously preserve stamp integrity and suppress background noise presents significant obstacles to the digitalization of document images. To tackle this challenge, this paper proposes StampGAN, a generative adversarial network designed specifically for stamp extraction from document images. The proposed network employs ResAUNet (Residual Asymmetric UNet) as the generator and PatchGAN as the discriminator. It further integrates a mod-ASPP (modified Atrous Spatial Pyramid Pooling) module and a co-att (collaborative attention) module, facilitating the generation of the binary mask. To verify the efficacy of the proposed model, a dataset was developed for stamp extraction, and experiments were conducted across different methods. With gains of 1.46%, 2.20%, and 0.84% respectively in IoU, SSIM and F1-score, the model surpasses the best-performing existing methods, demonstrating its effectiveness and superiority.
