UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030673085?origin=resultslist
AB  - This work proposes a Conditional Generative Adversarial Network (CGAN)-based framework for generating realistic 2D ultrasound images conditioned on 3D ultrasound probe (US-probe) poses, including both spatial position and orientation. The dataset, acquired from a breast ultrasound phantom (BB-03), consists of 2,656 multi-view B-mode images paired with 6-degree-of-freedom US-probe parameters, providing diverse US-probe movements and orientations to ensure comprehensive multi-view coverage for training. The US-probe positions and orientations are encoded using a positional encoding scheme that enables the model to learn spatial relationships between probe placement and internal anatomy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model generates anatomically consistent grayscale ultrasound slices across diverse probe poses. Moreover, the results indicate that the proposed positional encoding method enhances the quality and realism of the synthesized images, showing improved alignment with US-probe position and orientation compared to a baseline approach. This is further reflected by consistently high Pose-Consistency Scores (PCS) ranging from 0.8766 to 0.8950. In addition, quantitative image quality assessments indicate that the synthesized images achieve performance close to the acceptable ranges across standard ultrasound evaluation metrics. Although further refinements are needed to enhance realism, these findings highlight the potential of CGAN-based frameworks for ultrasound image synthesis, where preserving both probe-dependent spatial positioning and anatomical structural consistency remains essential. ©2025 IEEE.
