AB  - This paper introduces MetaLLMetro, a Virtual Reality (VR) subway station system that integrates Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance user navigation and realtime traffic information acquisition. Navigating underground subway stations remains challenging, particularly when the stations are complex and difficult to locate the correct directions or platforms. To overcome these limitations, we propose a VR platform where a fine-tuned LLM interprets users' natural language requests, accurately infers their intended destinations, and dynamically generates navigation paths within a virtual 3D subway environment. By fine-tuning the LLM on transit-specific tasks and queries, MetaLLMetro delivers a more accurate and intuitive navigation experience, effectively bridging the gap between ambiguous user input and precise, actionable spatial directions. Furthermore, our system leverages LLMdriven queries to interface with the Google Maps API, offering users a routing assistant that provides real-time geographic context and step-by-step navigation guidance. © 2025 IEEE.
