AB  - The chapter presents the concept of an ecosystem of neuro-coworkers within an educational institution as an innovative approach to integrating large language models (LLMs) into the processes of learning, teaching, and administrative management. It introduces a concept of a multi-agent architecture that envisions the interaction of various types of neuro-coworkers forming a unified cognitive system of the institution. The prospects of establishing inter-university networks that facilitate knowledge exchange among educational institutions through a centralized system of federated model training are examined. Approaches are proposed to address the issues of function duplication and inconsistency among neuro-coworkers by implementing a unified cognitive core with enhanced capabilities for personalizing the educational process. The use of 3D world generation technologies is discussed as a means for visualizing educational material and applying knowledge in practice, particularly in literature, physics, and mathematics. Special attention is given to the role of artificial general intelligence (AGI) in the formation of superknowledge and the transformation of the educational process at the level of researcher training, with a forecasted paradigm shift in the interaction between human intelligence and AI. The necessity of developing cognitive assistants for interpreting and integrating knowledge generated by AI is substantiated, along with the need to design symbiotic learning systems for the effective utilization of AGI capabilities in future scientific and educational environments. © 2025 River Publishers. All rights reserved.
