TI  - The Restorative Effects of AI-Generated Virtual Nature on Stress Recovery
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Conference on Construction Engineering and Project Management
SP  - 458
EP  - 465
DO  - 10.6106/ICCEPM.2025.0458
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050312896?origin=resultslist
AB  - The application of virtual reality (VR) technology in the field of mental health is increasingly widespread, particularly with natural environment simulations showing potential for stress reduction. Stress, as a common problem facing modern individuals, makes effective stress management interventions particularly valuable for personal health outcomes. The rapid development of generative artificial intelligence (AI) has provided new possibilities for VR experiences through its capability to create realistic natural images. Despite the rapid development of generative AI, there is still a lack of systematic research on the psychological impacts of AI-generated natural scenarios in VR. In particular, the application potential of such scenarios for stress intervention requires further exploration. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of VR experiences in natural scenarios created by generative AI for stress recovery. Through an experimental design measuring psychological indicators, this study assessed stress levels before and after exposure to AI-generated virtual natural scenarios. The result demonstrates that VR experiences in natural scenarios created by generative AI can effectively reduce the stress levels of subjects, while also offering significant advantages in time and cost. The contribution of the study is to provide new insights into the application of VR and generative AI technologies in human-centered interventions, offering an innovative approach to stress management for individuals. © 2025, Korea Institute of Construction Engineering and Management. All rights reserved.
