TI  - Toward Listening 4.0: Technology-Based Instruction, Research, and Assessment
PY  - 2025
T2  - The Handbook of Second Language Listening
SP  - 348
EP  - 362
DO  - 10.1002/9781394312375.ch25
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032053892?origin=resultslist
AB  - Second language (L2) listening has gone through four stages analogous to the development of the Internet (Web 1.0–4.0). In Listening 1.0 (before 1960), listening instruction was primarily intuition-based, and minimal technology was used. In Listening 2.0 (1961–1995), theoretical developments in L2 acquisition led to significant advances in listening instruction, and technology such as cassette recorders and laser discs was increasingly used to deliver listening materials. In Listening 3.0 (1996–2015), extensive authentic and multimodal resources on the Internet were integrated into listening instruction, enriching learners’ experience and creating opportunities for autonomous and self-directed learning outside the classroom. In Listening 4.0 (2016–future), advanced technologies, such as artificial intelligence and extended reality, are driving the transformation of listening instruction from a teacher-centered, classroom-based instruction to an individualized, contextualized, and interactive system. In addition to advances in pedagogical practice, research and assessment in Listening 4.0 are also witnessing major paradigm shifts characterized by the growing adoption of cognitive and neurocognitive methodologies. Assessment in Listening 4.0 is moving toward a multimodal, integrated, and interactive approach. This chapter reviews the historical, current, and future development of listening and outlines Listening 4.0 in an era of unprecedented technological advancement. © 2026 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc. All rights reserved.
