DO  - 10.1109/CGIP66596.2025.00024
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032944748?origin=resultslist
AB  - In this study, we propose a self-sustainable VR-AI data pipeline system that can generate quality training data through limited user interactions in virtual reality environments, effectively freeing us from the reliance on voluminous external datasets. The framework integrates a Transformer-based sparse interaction encoder, a generative variational self-encoder in the latent space, and an independently-run adversarial augmentation module to collaboratively generate synthetic samples that perfectly fit the context. The encoder focuses on capturing the temporal dynamics of user inputs, the variational self-encoder transforms them into a structured latent representation system, which lays the foundation for diversified sample generation, and the synthesized outputs are evaluated for authenticity with the help of a Wasserstein GAN discriminator, which realizes a customizable and intelligent art creation tool. This synthetic data underpins subsequent tasks such as 3D mesh generation techniques based on graph neural networks for predicting vertex displacements from optimized cases. We employ rotational position encoding techniques in the Transformer architecture, and the discriminator module applies a spectral normalization strategy to ensure stable system performance. The whole process creatively integrates temporal modeling, latent space generation, and adversarial optimization mechanisms to reduce data dependency while maintaining artistic freedom. Experiments show that the scheme can efficiently produce realistic and diverse training data, significantly improve the performance level of AI technology in the VR field, and open up new possibilities for scaled-up, user-oriented creative engineering in virtual space. © 2025 IEEE.
