AB  - The importance of students’ practical abilities in addition to their theoretical knowledge has grown as a result of educational changes. In response, the given paper introduces a deep learning (DL)-powered virtual reality (VR) practice framework that considers the aspect of facial expression recognition (FER) to monitor the progress of students in online learning. The framework integrates an attention-based convolutional neural network (CNN) and a generative adversarial network (GAN) for enhanced FER. Attention-based CNN enhances the framework’s performance by focusing on spatial and channel-wise features based on the region of interest (ROI) approach, whereas GAN captures temporal dependencies for analyzing changes in facial expressions during online learning in educational systems. Also, GAN lowers image positioning calibration errors and improves the average import rate of the virtual environment. The performance of the proposed framework is evaluated on two FER datasets, namely the Facial Expression Research Group Database (FERG-DB) and Dynamic Facial Expression in the Wild (DFEW), based on performance metrics such as accuracy (%), precision (%), recall (%), and F1 score (%). The results show that the proposed framework surpasses existing models, thus allowing students to acquire computer-aided design (CAD) models more actively and hone their practical abilities. © Bharati Vidyapeeth's Institute of Computer Applications and Management 2025.
