AB  - Addressing the dual challenges in traditional foreign language education—the lack of authentic intercultural practice scenarios and the high affective filter in learners—this study explores a human-AI collaborative teaching model integrating Large Language Models and Virtual Reality technologies. We developed an AI virtual instructor system incorporating an affective dialogue management model based on the Markov Decision Process (MDP), embedded within high-fidelity VR intercultural scenarios (e.g., business negotiations, social banquets) pre-designed with cultural conflict trigger points. An 8-week controlled experiment with 120 non-English major university students revealed that the experimental group significantly outperformed the traditional multimedia teaching group in intercultural sensitivity (t=6.82, p<0.001) and language fluency. Furthermore, heart rate monitoring data indicated that the experimental group exhibited a significantly lower average heart rate (M=72.3 bpm) during interactions compared to the control group's average heart rate while watching videos (M=78.6 bpm) (t=4.21, p<0.01), suggesting reduced state anxiety. The findings demonstrate that the MDP-driven AI virtual instructor, provides an algorithmic framework for dynamically adjusting teaching strategies (e.g., switching from “cultural correction” to “emotional support”), offering initial evidence for its potential to create a low-anxiety, high-immersion “safe-fail” practice environment. However, as this study employed a holistic intervention package combining VR immersion, interactivity, and AI feedback strategies, the unique contribution of the MDP-driven mechanism requires further investigation. This human-AI collaboration model offers a new perspective on leveraging technology to address the scarcity of authentic intercultural communication opportunities, providing new theoretical and practical pathways for the digital transformation of foreign language education.
