TI  - Act-Env matching: activity and context-aware avatar positioning for telepresence in heterogeneous environments
PY  - 2026
T2  - Frontiers in Virtual Reality
VL  - 7
DO  - 10.3389/frvir.2026.1791810
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049088652?origin=resultslist
AB  - Augmented reality (AR) telepresence with life-sized avatars combines the advantages of both face-to-face interaction and remote communication. For such experiences to feel natural, avatars must be positioned such that they appear plausible in the local environment. This problem has attracted attention for some time, and various methods have been proposed. However, most existing AR telepresence systems assume that local and remote environments share similar spatial layouts, thereby limiting their applicability in real-world settings. This study proposes Act-Env Matching, an activity- and context-aware avatar positioning method that uses an MLLM to automatically determine the position and orientation of a life-sized avatar using only remote user’s activity label and a video of the local environment, without requiring spatial similarity between the two environments. Unlike previous methods, which are designed to place the avatar so that the relative relationship between the remote user and the environmental context is represented in the local environment, our method is designed to suggest avatar placements suited to the local environment given an activity label. The system first extracts keyframes from the video, capturing as many representative features of the environment as possible. Then, it uses a multimodal large language model with these extracted keyframes and the remote user’s activity label to select one keyframe that includes an activity- and context-matched scene, and then determines two pixel locations in the keyframe that indicate the position and facing direction of the avatar. Finally, the proposed system back-projects these pixel locations into 3D coordinates in the local environment using depth maps and camera parameters. Results from two experiments and a real-environment demonstration suggest that activity- and context-matched placements may be perceived as appropriate and preferable by the users. Overall, the results suggest that Act-Env Matching may support plausible activity-aligned placement of life-sized AR telepresence avatars across diverse indoor environments without requiring similar spatial layouts between local and remote environments. Copyright © 2026 Deguchi, Taguchi, Hamazaki, Takeyama, Sakai, Shimura, Yonezawa and Kawaguchi.
