PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 223
EP  - 228
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3820949
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3820949
AB  - With the rapid popularization of interactive systems (e.g., e-commerce platforms, social media, and content streaming services), AI recommendation models have become core components to address information overload. However, traditional recommendation algorithms tend to over-fit user historical preferences, leading to the formation of information cocoons, which not only limit users’ access to diverse information but also reduce long-term user engagement. To solve this problem, this paper proposes an adaptive AI recommendation model (AARM) that integrates dynamic user preference modeling, diversity optimization, and real-time feedback adjustment. The model first captures the temporal evolution of user interests through a time-aware preference encoder, then introduces a diversity constraint mechanism to balance recommendation accuracy and content diversity, and finally realizes adaptive adjustment of recommendation strategies based on real-time user interaction feedback. Experimental results on MovieLens and Amazon Reviews datasets show that compared with traditional collaborative filtering, content-based recommendation, and LLM-enhanced hybrid models, the proposed AARM improves user click-through rate (CTR) by 12.3% and 9.7% respectively, increases recommendation diversity by 21.5% and 18.9%, and effectively alleviates the information cocoon effect while ensuring user experience. This study provides a feasible solution for building user-centric, diverse, and adaptive recommendation systems in interactive scenarios.
