AB  - VR applications often exclude individuals with visual impairments, creating a significant accessibility gap. We present a real-time assistive framework integrating Vision Large Language Models (VLLMs) to interpret complex visual scenes. The system generates context-aware audio descriptions combined with haptic collision feedback, translating visual data into perceptible patterns. A user study with blind (N=3) and low-vision (N=2) participants was conducted in simulated clinic and classroom environments. Findings show that AI-driven assistance significantly enhanced spatial awareness, independence, and task performance. Qualitative results confirmed reduced cognitive load and heightened presence, providing a robust, VLLM-powered path for designing inclusive immersive systems. © 2026 IEEE.
