TI  - Archival Fragments to Immersive Worlds: An AI-assisted Workflow for the Digital Reconstruction of Lost Architectural Heritage
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Education and Research in Computer Aided Architectural Design in Europe
VL  - 3
SP  - 317
EP  - 326
DO  - 10.52842/conf.ecaade.2026.3.317
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051222093?origin=resultslist
AB  - This paper presents and verifies an AI-enhanced workflow for the digital reconstruction of lost urban heritage in contexts where archival documentation is scarce, often limited to low-quality photographs, historical maps, and fragmented architectural drawings. The workflow systematically addresses challenges of precision and interpretation occurring due to gaps and inconsistencies within such sources while enabling efficient three-dimensional modelling and immersive visualisation. The proposed four-phase workflow begins with archival research and evidence collection, followed by AI-assisted image enhancement and colourisation to complete missing architectural details. Processed imagery is then translated into three-dimensional geometry through image-to-mesh techniques and finally integrated into interactive virtual reality environments, allowing users to immerse into reconstructed urban spaces. The method is tested in a destroyed late 19th-century neighbourhood around the Arch ofGalerius in late 19th-century Thessaloniki's historic centre. Findings demonstrate that, despite challenges of precision and interpretation, the workflow offers a replicable approach for reconstructing lost architectural heritage and advancing immersive digital historiography. © 2026, Education and research in Computer Aided Architectural Design in Europ. All rights reserved.
