AB  - With the development of new media technologies, virtual reality (VR) has increasingly become an important medium for contemporary artistic expression. However, many existing VR art practices remain visually centered and lack sufficient multisensory narrative and emotional engagement. To address this limitation, this paper explores VR as an artistic language through the integration of ecological themes and multisensory interaction. Taking the self-developed “Five Elements” Eco-VR Experience Space as a case study, the study examines how visual, auditory, and tactile interactions can be systematically combined to construct an immersive and emotionally resonant experience. Guided by user-centered design principles and supported by techniques such as 3D modeling, spatial audio, haptic feedback, and procedural animation, the research demonstrates that VR can function not merely as a technical medium but as a generative language for artistic expression. The study provides a practice-oriented reference for immersive art creation and ecological communication. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
