EP  - 201
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3820945
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3820945
AB  - Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is widely used in clinical diagnosis due to its superior soft-tissue contrast and the absence of ionizing radiation. However, the spatial resolution of acquired MRI is often constrained by scan time and signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), leading to blurred details and missing high-frequency information, which can hinder accurate interpretation. MRI super-resolution aims to reconstruct high-resolution (HR) images from low-resolution (LR) inputs without additional scanning cost, thereby improving image quality while maintaining clinical throughput. To address the training instability and insufficient detail recovery commonly observed in conventional deep-learning super-resolution methods, this paper proposes an improved generative adversarial network (GAN) named SRGAN-GA. Built upon SRGAN, the proposed framework first introduces the Wasserstein distance with gradient penalty (SRGAN-GP) to stabilize adversarial training, and then incorporates an attention mechanism and a conditional discrimination strategy (SRGAN-GA) to enhance local structural and fine-detail restoration in MRI. Experimental results on an MRI dataset demonstrate that SRGAN-GA achieves the best performance, reaching 34.22 dB in PSNR and 0.917 in SSIM under SRF = 2, outperforming baseline models. The proposed method provides an efficient and stable solution for MRI super-resolution with promising potential for clinical image-quality enhancement.
