SN  - 2165-9567
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044385846?origin=resultslist
AB  - VR training benefits greatly from realistic non-player characters that imitate real people, enabling higher levels of empathy and immersion. This makes conversational agents-and thus Large Language Models (LLMs) - promising tools, but they still often lack the control and reliability required in educational contexts. This work explores how large language models can be used to produce natural dialogue in educational simulations while maintaining specific pedagogical goals and narrative control. We present an early-stage design of a controlled LLM-powered dialogue framework for use in educational VR simulations. The system utilizes ChatGPT to generate varied conversations while integrating author-defined narrative structures, boundaries, and safeguards to mitigate bias, hallucinations, and inappropriate content. A speech-based interaction loop, coupled with user intent classification and authored dialogue branching, retains believable AI-interaction while preserving pedagogical usefulness. The findings indicate that LLMs can be adapted into reliable and modular VR tools when paired with narrative constraints. Beyond ensuring safe interaction, the added control enables AI-generated dialogue to directly influence the environment through variables and scripted events. This establishes a foundation for simulations - both educational and game-based - that employ LLM-controlled characters capable of diverse, natural dialogue while remaining anchored to their designed roles and progression within the scenario. © 2026 IEEE.
