TI  - Beyond Reactive Dialogue: Designing Emotionally Situated Contexts for AI-Enabled VR Nursing Training
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Computer Science
VL  - 16746 LNAI
SP  - 436
EP  - 454
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-31048-4_27
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046497430?origin=resultslist
AB  - Virtual reality simulations powered by large language models (LLMs) are increasingly adopted for professional communication training. However, LLM-driven non-player characters (NPCs) exhibit structural limitations such as emotional drift and benevolence bias, often failing to sustain the high-arousal negative states required for realistic conflict scenarios. Such premature emotional neutralization risks negative transfer by encouraging unrealistic expectations that clinical distress will resolve quickly without meaningful intervention. To address this limitation, we introduce the Emotional Continuity Mechanism (ECM), a modular architecture that treats emotional inertia as a controllable design variable. ECM employs a Hybrid Semantic–Numerical Steering strategy that decouples internal emotional state maintenance from dialogue generation, enabling affective constraints to persist across multi-turn interactions without model fine-tuning. We evaluate ECM through a controlled ablation study using standardized user agents in normal and high-conflict nursing communication scenarios. Results from a dual-evaluator framework demonstrate that ECM significantly reduces emotional decay and preserves logically consistent affective trajectories, whereas baseline models exhibit spontaneous emotional “self-healing,” thereby enhancing interactional realism and the fidelity of emotionally demanding training simulations. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
