SP  - 153
EP  - 161
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3777730.3777756
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3777730.3777756
AB  - Forgetting is the complementary process to memory, enabling both biological and artificial systems to prioritize relevant information while discarding outdated or redundant data. In large language models (LLMs), forgetting occurs through computational mechanisms such as attention bottlenecks, catastrophic interference, and optimization dynamics, mirroring neurobiological processes such as synaptic decay and memory consolidation. While existing research has extensively studied memory mechanisms in LLMs, there remains a critical gap in systematically analyzing forgetting phenomena and their implications for AI system design. This study presents a comprehensive review of forgetting mechanisms in LLMs through a neuroscience-inspired lens. This study first establishes parallels between human forgetting processes and computational forgetting patterns in LLMs. Building on these foundations, this study proposes a novel taxonomy that classifies LLM forgetting across four key dimensions. This taxonomy is further supported by the introduction of quantitative evaluation metrics to provide additional evidence. Furthermore, this study demonstrates how controlled forgetting can be transformed from a limitation into a design feature through bio-inspired optimization strategies. These approaches enable critical applications in privacy-compliant AI, adaptive education systems, and sustainable computing. The study concludes by identifying open challenges in generalization, ethical governance of machine forgetting, and computational efficiency, while outlining future directions for developing LLMs with human-like memory adaptability.
