AB  - DoRA advances parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) by decomposing weights into magnitude and direction components, motivated by the observation that full fine-tuning exhibits strong negative correlation (ρ = −0.62) between their updates. However, DoRA’s implicit coupling achieves only weak correlation (ρ = −0.31), leaving substantial synergistic potential unrealized. We identify this magnitude-direction coordination gap as the fundamental bottleneck limiting DoRA’s efficiency. We propose explicit bi-level collaborative reinforcement that actively monitors and constrains the correlation toward full fine-tuning targets through adaptive loss weighting. The mechanism operates at two granularities: block-level constraints ensure global coordination across Transformer layers, while layer-level constraints provide targeted correction for severely deviating projection matrices. Experiments on vision-language retrieval benchmarks (Flickr30k [16], MSCOCO [11], Visual Genome [8]) with CLIP [19] and BLIP [9] models show our method improves Recall@1 by 2.7-3.2% over DoRA while using identical trainable parameters (less than 1.1%), achieving magnitude-direction correlation of ρ = −0.53 that substantially closes the gap toward full fine-tuning’s ρ = −0.62.
