AB  - This chapter presents four innovative educational initiatives at the University of Michigan's Department of Nuclear Engineering and Radiological Sciences aimed at transforming nuclear engineering education to encourage creativity and broader youth and public participation. These initiatives, created in the context of increasing global commitments to nuclear energy expansion and rapid technological advancements, include four courses (three of which are newly developed): Socially Engaged Design of Nuclear Energy Technologies, Nuclear Technology, Policy, and Society, a redesigned introductory nuclear engineering course incorporating artistic elements, and Harper Academy—a summer program for high school students. These programs emphasize creativity, public engagement, ethics, and engineering stewardship, fostering a sociotechnical approach to engineering education. By integrating participatory design and creative problem-solving across these courses, we aim to produce engineers capable of anticipating the societal impacts of their work. Preliminary feedback suggests strong student engagement and a potential for these models to be expanded and adapted for wider application in engineering education beyond our home department, particularly within the growing nuclear sector. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
