AB  - Public input in street micro-renewal is usually collected through surveys or consultation workshops, making it hard for laypeople to translate situated experience into proposals. We introduce CityCure, an in-situ mixed reality system that lets citizens capture streetscapes, use an LLM to structure voice input into design intent, and generate context-aware visual variants via image-to-image AI. In a Hong Kong deployment, interviews and questionnaires show how CityCure helps participants move from vague impressions to actionable goals. Thematic analysis revealed three design needs: fine-grained control to negotiate outputs; responsive voice input paired with asynchronous cross-device refinement; and social infrastructure to transform individual expression into collective practice. These findings highlight that generative output can become a civic proposal when people can iteratively steer, verify, and socialize it. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
