TI  - Conversational AI for Safety-Critical Virtual and Extended Reality: Intelligent Agents Across Health Informatics, Navigation, and Urban Sensing
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 IEEE International Conference on Artificial Intelligence Testing, AITest 2026
SP  - 207
EP  - 210
DO  - 10.1109/AITest70988.2026.00040
SN  - 2835-3560
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049695300?origin=resultslist
AB  - The integration of large language model (LLM)-driven conversational AI with virtual, augmented, and extended reality (VR/AR/XR) is producing a new class of embodied agents capable of perceiving spatial context, interpreting user intent, and acting on the physical world through device-native function calls. This paper presents the design, implementation, and preliminary qualitative evaluation of three AI-powered intelligent agents across three Unity-based XR applications evaluated against a unified five-dimensional AI testing framework. The first is a VR Mental Health Companion on Meta Quest 3, in which AI-driven non-player characters facilitate cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) dialogue, mindfulness guidance, and somatic yoga training, augmented by EEG-based stress monitoring and pose estimation. The second is an AR indoor evacuation navigator deployed on HoloLens 2 and smart devices, where a retrieval-augmented conversational agent answers natural language safety queries and render real-time AR path overlays. The third is a multimodal urban sensing assistant on Android that provides natural language access to a spatiotemporal event constructed from on-device object detection, face recognition, emotion classification, and GPS-tagged parking data. Across all three systems, we document a five-dimensional evaluation framework spanning response correctness, multi-turn coherence, adversarial robustness, on-device latency, and demographic fairness. For the first three dimensions, we report qualitative observations drawn from LLM-generated test queries, document-grounded response review, and structured adversarial probing conducted across all three applications. Together, these contributions establish a reusable testing methodology and a deployment blueprint for LLM-driven agents in safety-critical Unity XR pipelines. © 2026 IEEE.
