TI  - Designing Perceptually Grounded AI-Powered Systems for Haptic Interaction
PY  - 2026
T2  - Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems - Proceedings
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3772363.3799193
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038085897?origin=resultslist
AB  - Haptic feedback is essential for immersive environments, yet its design and creation remain significant bottlenecks. Unlike the visual and auditory domains, where large-scale datasets and generative AI have enabled scalable content creation, haptic design still relies heavily on manual, rule-based processes due to data scarcity and the subjective nature of touch. My research addresses this challenge by bridging human haptic perception with data-driven and generative approaches. Specifically, my work focuses on three interconnected research themes: (1) investigating multimodal haptic perception and characterizing its perceptual design space; (2) developing multimodal datasets and generative models for creating perceptually aligned haptic signals from other sensory modalities; and (3) designing real-time, in-situ authoring tools that integrate these models into virtual reality workflows to enable adaptive, context-aware haptic feedback. This paper reviews related work in haptic design, presents my objectives and approach, and outlines my progress and future directions toward perceptually grounded, AI-powered haptic design. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
