TI  - Development and evaluation of an AI-based virtual human communication simulation for nursing students’ communication with older patients: A mixed-methods study
PY  - 2026
T2  - Clinical Simulation in Nursing
VL  - 113
DO  - 10.1016/j.ecns.2026.101912
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031438829?origin=resultslist
AB  - Background: Communication with older patients is essential for person-centered nursing care, yet students often lack realistic practice opportunities. AI-based virtual human communication simulation (AI-VHCom) offers a scalable environment for developing therapeutic communication; therefore, this study developed the AI-VHCom and evaluated its effects on communication-related competencies and students’ learning experiences. Methods: A mixed-methods design was used with 30 senior nursing students who participated in eight AI-VHCom sessions grounded in Peplau's interpersonal relations theory. Emotional intelligence, empathy, critical thinking, and communication skills were measured pre and postintervention. Focus group interviews with 16 students explored learning experiences. Quantitative data were analyzed using paired t-tests, and qualitative data were examined through content analysis. Results: Emotional intelligence (p < .001), empathy (p < .001), critical thinking (p < .001), and communication skills (p < .001) improved significantly. Qualitative findings described enhanced rapport building, emotional support, clinical realism, adaptive strategies, and system limitations. Conclusions: The AI-VHCom significantly improved students’ emotional, cognitive, and communicative competencies, offering an immersive method for practicing therapeutic communication with older patients. © 2026
