TI  - Dissociable Perceptual Effects of Rendering and Animation in Expressive VR Avatars
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2026 ACM Symposium on Applied Perception
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3821409.3833883
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3821409.3833883
AB  - Designers of expressive VR avatars trade off rendering fidelity against animation quality, but it is unclear whether these factors shape perception through the same channel or through separate ones. We crossed avatar rendering (photorealistic MetaHuman vs. plain FLAME mesh) with animation method (FEELS, a generative transition model, vs. naive segment concatenation) in a within-subject VR study (N = 28), with each condition rated on six perceptual dimensions. Rendering produced a large effect on photorealism (d = 2.55) but small, non-significant effects on motion ratings; animation produced large effects on smoothness (d = 0.96) and timing (d = 0.98) but no detectable effect on photorealism. Naturalness and believability responded moderately to both factors, the two factors did not interact, and eeriness did not differ across conditions. We identify two factor-specific perceptual readouts—photorealism for rendering, smoothness and timing for animation—alongside aggregate quality judgments that respond to both. A fixed presentation order limits causal inference on the animation side, so we treat per-dimension animation effects as suggestive.
