TI  - Embodied AI Tutors in Extended Reality: A Systematic Review of the Effectiveness of Personalized Instruction and Affective Engagement
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 IEEE 5th International Conference on Intelligent Reality (ICIR)
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1109/ICIR70631.2026.11628320
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048765289?origin=resultslist
AB  - This systematic review investigates the impact of autonomous, embodied artificial intelligence (AI) tutors within extended reality (XR) environments on learning outcomes and affective engagement. Following the PRISMA 2020 guidelines, a comprehensive search was conducted across five databases for English-language journal and conference papers published between 2022 and 2026. Ten empirical studies were included in the review. Due to the heterogeneity in study design, intervention types, comparators, and outcomes, the findings were synthesized using descriptive statistics, and thematic analysis. Evidence indicates that embodied AI tutors can be beneficial for structured learning tasks that involve knowledge acquisition, procedural skills, retention, task performance, and guided problem solving. XR was found to add value in tasks necessitating spatial, situated, or simulation-based practice, although desktop systems may suffice for certain foundational outcomes. Affective findings were generally positive but less definitive; improvements were noted in engagement, confidence, usability, and satisfaction, yet intrinsic motivation was infrequently assessed, and real-time emotional adaptability was observed in only a few studies. Consequently, embodied AI tutors should be regarded as supplementary tools for structured learning. © 2026 IEEE.
