PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3786995.3787074
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105039870740?origin=resultslist
AB  - Virtual Reality (VR) applications are increasingly used to safeguard Intangible Cultural Heritage (ICH), yet many deployments emphasize static representation over participatory practice, keeping audiences as observers rather than makers. These often fail to capture or transmit the intricacies of highly specialized subcategories within a field, such as Beijing embroidery (Jingxiu), a subcategory of Chinese embroidery. In this paper, we explore how immersive VR, powered by generative AI, might transform Beijing embroidery from a static exhibit into a lived, co-creative experience that fosters understanding, agency, and attachment. We present EmbroidVR, a narrative-driven VR system in which users progress from knowledge immersion to AI-assisted creation to contextual application. We design three acts in which Beijing embroidery is practiced, namely royal palace, artisan workshop, and opera theatre. Our three-act framework combines sketch-conditioned diffusion with VR craft metaphors and a cultural transmission layer linking embroidery to their applications. We also outline a user-study plan for engagement, learning, and creative self-efficacy. This work is transferable to other ICH domains and paves the way for digitally supported ICH transmission in the long run. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
