AB  - With the increasing integration of multimodal interaction technologies and generative systems in contemporary art, emotion has become a structuring condition in artistic generation and experiential formation. However, affective computing frameworks centered on recognition and classification remain insufficient for explaining how emotion is transformed into aesthetic experience and meaning structures within artistic contexts. To address this limitation, this paper proposes emotion mapping as a methodological framework for analyzing multimodal interactive art. Rather than treating emotion as a directly identifiable psychological state, emotion mapping conceptualizes it as an experiential condition that is interpreted, encoded, and embedded into generative systems through artistic intervention. Based on an examination of multimodal affect-related technologies and their reconfiguration in artistic practice, this study develops a three-layer model—the perceptual, interpretive, and generative layers—to clarify how affective cues enter the system, undergo symbolic and cultural translation, and become operative within generative logic.
