AB  - Interfaces that use large language models (LLMs) enable users to express their preferences in natural language, eliminating the need to navigate complex menus. In virtual reality (VR) applications this approach could help to simplify setting privacy settings. To explore this potential, we conducted an exploratory workshop in a co-design setting using a Wizard-of-Oz prototype. We analysed participants' sentiments and identified their primary concerns regarding the trustworthiness, control and usability of voice-based privacy systems. Based on these findings, we propose three areas that we plan to investigate further: (1) providing VR-native verification and adjustment options, (2) offering contextual information to users when formulating a prompt, and (3) aligning system-level architecture with users' needs. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
