TI  - Feasibility projection guided layout constrained diffusion for automatic poster design
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2025 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 725
EP  - 731
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3777730.3777846
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3777730.3777846
AB  - For visual communication scenarios such as poster design, existing text-to-image diffusion models struggle to consistently meet professional layout specifications. Common issues include element overlap, margin violations, and insufficient text readability. This paper proposes a Feasibility Projection-Guided Layout Constrained Diffusion framework (FP-LCD). This framework uses a layout constraint encoder to formalize hard and soft constraints such as margins, collinearity/baseline, non-overlap, and whitespace into feasible regions and differentiable penalties. Denoising and projection are performed alternately during latent space sampling, and in-the-loop optical character recognition (OCR) readability and aesthetic priors are introduced for collaborative optimization. Experiments demonstrate that this framework significantly improves constraint compliance and visual quality on a standard test set: the feasibility rate increases to 89.7%, the overlap rate decreases to 0.029, the mean alignment error decreases to 5.2 px, the OCR confidence level increases to 0.91, and the double-blind preference success rate reaches 68.4%. These results demonstrate that deeply coupling generative modeling with constraint optimization can effectively bridge the gap between controllable and verifiable generation. This paper contributes by proposing a generalizable unified “projection-sampling-feedback” paradigm, constructing a layout-oriented multi-metric evaluation protocol and implementation details, and providing a verifiable and scalable technical path for the implementation of automated poster generation.
