AB  - Children’s artistic development plays a crucial role in fostering creativity, spatial reasoning, and self-expression. However, traditional drawing practices are often constrained by two-dimensional media, which may limit imaginative exploration and hinder children—particularly young learners—from developing strong creative self-efficacy. This study introduces a novel AI-driven augmented reality (AR) drawing headset, named SketchSpace, and explores how it empowers children’s artistic expression through adaptive scaffolding and collaborative spatial creation. Grounded in Vygotsky’s scaffolding theory and Bandura’s concept of self-efficacy, SketchSpace integrates real-time AI guidance, including step-by-step task decomposition, personalized constructive feedback, and intelligent tool recommendations. In addition, the design incorporates a collaborative AR environment that encourages peer co-creation and enhances social collaboration skills. Considering the physical safety risks associated with children’s use of AR devices, this study adopts a large language model (LLM)-driven Synthetic User Evaluation approach. Forty virtual participants (AI agents) simulating the cognitive characteristics of children aged 4–8 were constructed for simulation-based experiments. By quantitatively analyzing interaction behavior data, the results indicate that the AI-scaffolded simulation environment significantly improved the virtual participants’ creative self-efficacy scores, task persistence, and enjoyment. This research demonstrates the potential of AI-driven AR for rapid validation in the early stages of educational design and provides a novel simulation-based evaluation pathway for future research in children’s human–computer interaction. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
