AB  - Extended Reality (XR) environments are increas-ingly used for productivity tasks involving large language models (LLMs), such as document reading and analysis in multi-window workspaces. However, existing XR-based LLM systems face two major challenges: selecting relevant context from multiple con-current views and coordinating reasoning across multiple agents without overwhelming users. While prior gaze-aware approaches mainly focus on context selection, most systems still rely on single-agent architectures that struggle with document-level reasoning and temporal continuity. To address these limitations, we propose a gaze-grounded multi-agent orchestration framework for XR document understanding. The framework interprets users' gaze behaviors as probabilistic signals of cognitive states, including local comprehension, global exploration, and temporal connec-tion. Based on these signals, the system dynamically activates specialized reasoning agents, including a Gaze Agent, Global Agent, and Memory Agent. A synthesis module integrates outputs from multiple agents into unified responses to reduce information overload and role ambiguity. We present the system architecture and discuss its potential for improving interaction efficiency and adaptive reasoning support in XR document understanding environments. © 2026 IEEE.
