AB  - This study evaluated the impact of a gamified intervention integrating augmented reality (AR) and large language model (LLM)-powered chatbots on teaching Kepler's laws to first-year engineering students. A pretest-posttest design was implemented with 180 participants who took part in a 90-minute collaborative activity titled Discovering Planetary Motion. Organized into physical stations corresponding to each law, students used a custom AR application to visualize orbital mechanics and interacted with AI "space travelers"(chatbots trained using Ollama LLM) to solve guided challenges. Learning gains were assessed through parallel pre-and post-activity surveys featuring Likert-scale and multiple-choice questions. Results showed significant improvements in conceptual understanding: the proportion of students strongly agreeing that planetary motion is predictable increased by 11.4%, and strong disagreement was entirely eliminated. Comprehension of Kepler's second law (that planetary speed is greatest at perihelion) rose sharply from 78.4% to 97.7%. Understanding of the third law showed a measured increase from 87.5% to 92%, a 4.5% gain, while comprehension of Kepler's first law remained relatively stable, with a slight decrease from 96% to 94.9% after the activity. Students responded very positively to the intervention, with 88.0% indicating that AR increased their interest in learning and 94.9% reporting that the chatbots promoted reflective thinking. These findings demonstrate that combining gamified AR with conversational AI can markedly enhance the understanding of complex scientific concepts, providing an engaging and effective model for implementing active, immersive learning in STEM education. © 2026 IEEE.
