AB  - This survey examines how Generative AI (GenAI) influences the development of inclusive immersive learning environments built on Extended Reality (XR). While GenAI enhances XR through multimodal content generation, adaptive feedback, and real-time scene construction, its probabilistic nature introduces structural limitations—including reasoning inconsistency, contextual drift, multimodal desynchronization, strategic adaptation gaps, and ethical opacity—that can undermine reliability in multisensory, latency-sensitive contexts. Drawing on global XR market trends, policy frameworks, and cross-domain case studies in education, healthcare, creative practice, and civic participation, the survey analyzes how these limitations manifest in practice. It proposes inclusive design principles and a conceptual improvement framework integrating multimodal alignment learning, domain-specific fine-tuning, reinforcement-based adaptation, explainable generative interfaces, and hybrid edge–cloud architectures, while acknowledging associated computational trade-offs. To support empirical validation, the paper introduces a multidimensional evaluation model encompassing cross-modal fidelity, semantic integrity, cognitive stability, contextual continuity, and adaptive transparency. An illustrative application scenario and a minimal experimental protocol demonstrate how these dimensions may be operationalized. Overall, the survey provides an analytical and evaluative foundation for developing reliable and inclusive GenAI-enhanced XR learning systems for aging populations and creative communities. © 2026 KSII.
