PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821018
AB  - The rapid evolution of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is reshaping workflows for virtual world construction, complementing manual geometric modeling with latent-space-driven synthesis. This systematic review synthesizes empirical evidence regarding the effectiveness of Deep Learning (DL) architectures, specifically Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Diffusion Models, and Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), in facilitating immersive environment design and its subsequent impact on user cognition. Following the PRISMA protocol, 50 peer-reviewed empirical studies published between 2015 and 2025 were analyzed to evaluate the synergy between AI-driven environmental fidelity and human information processing. The findings, grounded in Dual-Coding Theory and Cognitive Load Theory, reveal a distinct divergence in system component efficacy: while DL-optimized “multimedia features” such as realistic rendering and intelligent soundscapes significantly enhance spatial presence and narrative comprehension, non-congruent “interactive functions” and excessive gamification frequently precipitate cognitive overload. The results indicate that AI-mediated environments bolster emergent spatial literacy and vocabulary acquisition primarily when “temporal and spatial contiguity” is maintained between generative assets and core task content. However, the “novelty effect” often masks a conflict between algorithmic complexity and the user's limited cognitive capacity, leading to fragmented retention in highly interactive but poorly structured environments. This review concludes by offering a theoretical framework for “narrative-first” design, providing critical guidelines for developers and educators to ensure that technological scaffolding enhances, rather than hampers, deep cognitive engagement in the era of Generative AI.
