TI  - Generative Placemaking: Co-creation of Cultural Memory Through a Collaborative Augmented Reality Narrative System in Foshan Historic Districts
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Computer Science
VL  - 16747 LNCS
SP  - 165
EP  - 181
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-30549-7_10
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044894151?origin=resultslist
AB  - Most digital cultural heritage applications still follow a “content-overlay” AR paradigm, superimposing predefined information onto physical sites for presentation and guided tours. This limits the narrative potential of physical media and constrains public agency in meaning-making. While prior work improves scene visualization and immersive tours, narratives remain expert-defined and hard to scale for open collaboration. To enable co-creative heritage storytelling, we designed and evaluated “Dynamic Mosaic,” a collaborative AR narrative system, and propose a generative placemaking interaction framework coupling “content generation—place experience—public participation.” Traditional mosaic craft was translated into a computational cultural interface as both spatial anchor and semantic base. In our experiment, physical mosaics triggered virtual characters, while participants uploaded images, texts, and story fragments; AIGC extended regionally grounded narratives that were assembled into dynamic semantic networks on a shared interface. Following a design science research paradigm, we recruited 80 participants (40 pairs; 48 female, 32 male; mean age 24.7, SD 3.5) for a simulation study with three conditions: physical-only (26), pure AR (26), and hybrid (28). Linear mixed-effects modeling showed the hybrid condition significantly outperformed single-media conditions in cultural learning, collaboration quality, and sense of place, yielding ~30% higher collaboration quality with no added cognitive load. Behavioral analysis further revealed spontaneous content re-creation beyond assigned tasks. We contribute a validated generative placemaking framework integrating traditional craft, AIGC, and AR, an experimental pipeline for collaborative narrative generation, and design evidence for transforming historic urban spaces into sustainable citizen-participatory digital cultural platforms. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
