DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-30860-3_24
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045694049?origin=resultslist
AB  - This study proposes a human-centered content development methodology for investigating how humans can effectively understand, control, and creatively collaborate with generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems in wearable computing and extended reality (XR)-based media environments. With AI transitioning from a production-oriented tool to a cognitive collaborator, the emphasis of creative systems shifts from automation to human competence—specifically how designers, educators, and creators refine their interactions with AI for greater creative intelligence, contextual awareness, and emotional depth. The proposed methodology introduces a three-phase framework comprising Human Understanding and Intent Modeling, AI-driven Generation and Adaptation, and XR Output Realization, focusing on human interpretive control and iterative feedback to guide and reshape AI-generated outputs. The framework is explored through scenario-based creative tasks and task-oriented activities, complemented by semi-structured interviews in which participants engage in representative text and image generation tasks situated within wearable and XR co-creation scenarios. These scenarios reflect interdisciplinary applications in cultural storytelling, multimedia production, and interactive XR installations using AI-enabled smart glasses and immersive interfaces. Qualitative analysis of participant responses and task outcomes suggests that the proposed approach supports AI interpretability, multimodal fluency, and embodied decision-making, contributing to more expressive, emotionally resonant, and contextually adaptive creative processes compared to automation-centric workflows. This study contributes to the emerging field of human–AI collaborative design by reframing wearable and XR systems as intelligent creative instruments and providing a methodological and educational foundation for training future creators to engage with AI as a collaborative partner that extends human imagination. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
