TI  - Human-in-the-Loop Rebar Inspection: Integrating Vision Foundation Model with Mixed Reality for Construction Quality Assurance
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 IEEE International Conference on Human-Machine Systems, ICHMS 2026
SP  - 452
EP  - 457
DO  - 10.1109/ICHMS69701.2026.11602218
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045597745?origin=resultslist
AB  - Rebar spacing must be precisely laid out before concrete is poured, yet current inspection relies on tape-measure readings, which are slow, error-prone, and leave no digital record. Automated computer-vision methods improve throughput but remain vulnerable to site clutter, and confine inspectors to screen-based workflows detached from the physical structure. This paper presents a human-in-the-loop Mixed Reality (MR) inspection framework that pairs a domain-adapted Segment Anything Model 3 (SAM3) with the Microsoft HoloLens 2. The system captures a rebar scene, segments each bar in real time, and maps the resulting 2D contours into 3D world coordinates via raycasting. Inspectors select any two rebars by air-tap gesture; the spacing is computed automatically and displayed as a holographic overlay anchored to the physical bars. Across 25 spacing pairs and 75 measurements on a controlled rebar mockup, the system achieved a mean relative error of 2.52% (95% CI 2.46%-2.58%) and and end-to-end latency of 2.8 s, confirming suitability for on-site quality control. © 2026 IEEE.
