TI  - Igniting Heritage: A Framework for AI-Enhanced XR Documentary Design of Chinese Fire-Based ICH Performances
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Computer Science
VL  - 16726 LNCS
SP  - 415
EP  - 432
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-30073-7_25
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043952594?origin=resultslist
AB  - Chinese fire-based Intangible Cultural Heritage (ICH), such as the vigorous “Fire Qilin” dances, faces a profound paradox: its cultural power is rooted in the visceral, dangerous, and ephemeral nature of live fire, yet these very attributes accelerate its decline. Prevailing digital preservation methods, predominantly two-dimensional video, fail to capture the multisensory, embodied, and spatially-grounded essence of such rituals, often reducing them to passive spectacles. This paper argues for a paradigm shift from archival replication to empathetic, narrative-driven re-living. We propose and demonstrate a practical pathway that synergizes XR Virtual Production and Generative AI to create culturally resonant narrative films. Grounded in the Digital Humanities principles of cultural empathy and experiential authenticity, our two-stage workflow involves: 1) Cultural Sensing and Controlled Field Building, using XR stages to safely reconstruct ritual performances within authentic digital settings; and 2) Generative Augmentation and Narrative Synthesis, where AI-generated visual elements (e.g., digital fireworks) are created under community guidance to amplify cultural meaning, followed by cinematic storytelling. Through a case study of the “Dongchong Fire Qilin,” we produced a short film that was validated by the inheritor Master Mai as shocking and spectacular, confirming it captured the tradition’s true spirit. This work contributes a validated, humanistic approach to digital heritage, showcasing how virtual production and generative AI can serve as powerful tools for creative fidelity and sustainable cultural transmission, while critically engaging with the ethics of narrative sovereignty and digital interpretation. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
KW  - Cultural Empathy
KW  - Digital Humanities
