AB  - This paper presents the research efforts of a doctoral program aimed at enabling enhanced, more natural and effective interactive experiences within immersive multimedia environments. The research builds on the development of an immersive virtual reconstruction of a demolished 16th-century theater, which serves as a comprehensive research framework to build upon. The work is structured around three main connected pillars: the multimodal reconstruction of historical heritage, the evaluation of interaction mechanics including shared multiuser visits, and the integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) to exploit its potential within immersive media. Along this path, a controlled study comparing input and presentation methods for Virtual Reality (VR) user interfaces has been conducted, producing findings on the trade-offs between interaction methods and presence. Building on this work, planned directions include expanding the application with holoportation technologies, conducting further user evaluations, and investigating how generative AI could be applied within immersive media. This paper describes the completed work, the ongoing activities, and the planned directions. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
