AB  - In this paper, we discuss the potential of shared Cross-Reality (CR) for individuals with Neurodevelopmental Disorders (NDDs) and describe the development and preliminary evaluation of a system that implements this approach, called Social Match UP (SMUP). SMUP integrates Virtual Reality (VR) and Augmented Reality (AR) within a single cooperative game, allowing seamless transitions between the two forms of virtuality. Additionally, it incorporates a conversational assistant powered by a Large Language Model (LLM) to provide real-time guidance, encourage communication, and promote user engagement. An exploratory study was conducted with four participants diagnosed with NDDs, focusing on the usability of CR transitions and users' perceptions of the conversational assistant. Given the small sample size, results are intended to inform design implications rather than establish statistical significance. While the ability to switch between VR and AR was acknowledged as a valuable feature, participants found the transition difficult to use, occasionally disrupting their immersion. VR was generally preferred over AR, though it remains unclear whether this preference was due to usability challenges related to reality switching. The results also indicate that the LLM-powered assistant was perceived as friendly and engaging, fostering a sense of presence, particularly in VR settings, where it felt more realistic and immersive. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
