VL  - 190
DO  - 10.1016/j.autcon.2026.107105
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042282989?origin=resultslist
AB  - Most Mixed Reality(MR) systems for prefabricated construction remain visualization-centric and weakly coupled to perception and planning. This paper presents Mixed Reality Assembly Orchestration (MRAO), integrating BIM-derived assembly knowledge, geometry-based zero-shot recognition, LLM planning, MR guidance, and a cable-driven actuation backend for assisted handling. Rhino exports a knowledge base and signatures; runtime recognition updates state, and LLM proposals are gated by whitelists plus rule and geometry validators, then executed via MR-only or MR+Robot backends. Image-level recognition reaches 50.0%–87.5% across five component families with tens-of-milliseconds per-image processing. A CLT-situated guardrail stress test injects 360 violation cases and blocks 100.0% with 0–1ms median routing latency (max 14ms). In a 39-component ablation, an unconstrained planner increases assembly time to 609.9s vs 102.3s baseline, while whitelist constraints and pose repair reduce it to 112.4s. Physical user trials show 86.0% fewer corrections and 68.2% fewer help requests than 2D drawings. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
