SP  - 268
EP  - 275
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3820956
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3820956
AB  - With the development of the digital cultural industry, the digital preservation and intelligent design of traditional cultural patterns have gradually become an important interdisciplinary field in computer vision and cultural heritage research. However, existing image generation methods still suffer from limitations in data scale, insufficient cultural semantic expression, and low style transfer stability in intangible cultural heritage pattern generation tasks. To address these issues, this paper proposes an intangible cultural heritage pattern generation method that combines multimodal Transformer and contrastive learning mechanisms. This method extracts cultural symbols and structural information from traditional patterns by constructing a cultural feature encoder, and uses a multimodal Transformer model to model the global structural relationships of the patterns. Simultaneously, a contrastive learning mechanism is introduced to optimize the feature space distribution, thereby enhancing the model's ability to express cultural semantic features. Experiments are conducted on a constructed intangible cultural heritage pattern dataset. The results show that compared with mainstream generative models such as CycleGAN and StyleGAN2, the proposed method achieves significant improvements in FID, SSIM, and cultural consistency scores. The generated patterns exhibit better stability and consistency in both visual quality and cultural symbol expression. The research results demonstrate that combining cultural semantic modeling with deep generative models can effectively improve the quality of traditional pattern generation. This study provides a new technical framework for the intelligent generation and style transfer of intangible cultural heritage patterns, and offers valuable technical references for digital cultural heritage protection and cultural and creative design.
