TI  - Post-digital pedagogical models: Implementing Artificial Intelligence (Al) and Augmented Reality (AR) through experiential and reflective practice in architectural education
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Education and Research in Computer Aided Architectural Design in Europe
VL  - 3
SP  - 139
EP  - 148
DO  - 10.52842/conf.ecaade.2026.3.139
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051205284?origin=resultslist
AB  - This research critically assesses the systematic integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Augmented Reality (AR) within architectural design pedagogy, aiming to facilitate the transition from digital concepts to built forms. Despite the growing presence of these tools professionally, their holistic incorporation in education remains limited. The study employs a post-digital pedagogical paradigm rooted in constructivist learning theories, specifically drawing on Kolb’s experiential learning cycle and Schon’s reflective practitioner concept. The methodology utilizes two distinct case studies: a twelve-week MArch design studio focusing on AI ideation andAR construction simulation in bamboo architecture, and a two-week MArch elective centred on multi-objective optimisation and 1:1 AR holographic prototyping for structural assembly. The research demonstrates that embedding AI and AR within iterative, reflexive workflows operationalises constructivist approaches, fostering active experimentation and tactile engagement. Findings indicate that while AI generates novel forms, the pedagogical challenge shifts to cultivating critical judgment. On the contrary, AR successfully bridges the digital-to-physical gap, simplifying access to advanced construction methodologies, expanding the architectural design and construction solution space. This approach develops the critical thinking and adaptive expertise required for complex contemporary design challenges. © 2026, Education and research in Computer Aided Architectural Design in Europ. All rights reserved.
