TI  - Procedural Learning through VR-Based Embodied Conversational Agents for Construction Safety Education
PY  - 2026
T2  - Construction Research Congress 2026: Advanced Technologies, Artificial Intelligence, and Data Analytics in Construction - Selected papers from Construction Research Congress 2026
VL  - 1
SP  - 806
EP  - 816
DO  - 10.1061/9780784486962.078
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049166356?origin=resultslist
AB  - Construction sites are among the most hazardous work environments, highlighting the critical need for effective procedural skill development. Traditional training predominantly focuses on delivering declarative knowledge while largely neglecting procedural learning to perform tasks effectively under real-world conditions. Virtual Reality (VR) has emerged as a promising medium by enabling experiential learning in controlled environments, while recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have introduced new possibilities for intelligent, language-driven instruction within immersive settings. Existing VR-conversational systems lack the capability to deliver physical demonstrations that are precisely driven by verbal context, which is essential for procedural learning in construction safety, where workers must develop integrated motor-cognitive skills. This paper presents a novel integration of Embodied Conversational Agents (ECAs) with VR environments to enable systematic procedural learning for construction safety education. The framework combines LLM-driven dialogue with physically embodied demonstrations, allowing avatars to guide learners while performing context-sensitive safety procedures. A welding safety case study demonstrates the system's practical application, where ECAs provide real-time, adaptive instruction and physically interact with the environment. Preliminary expert evaluation indicates that this approach enhances responsiveness, interactivity, and procedural knowledge acquisition. The study contributes a scalable methodology for context-aware safety training in high-risk construction domains. © 2026 ASCE.
