AB  - In the global business landscape, contract review has long relied on manual processes, leading to issues such as low efficiency, high costs, and heightened legal and financial risks. Existing AI-assisted approaches—whether rule-based templates, statistical machine learning, or end-to-end LLMs—suffer from limitations such as poor generalization, dependence on annotated data, or uncontrollable outputs, and they fail to deconstruct the cognitive process of review in a refined manner. This paper introduces an AI Agent framework to model contract review as a multi-agent task flow and develops an intelligent review system. The process is decomposed into subtasks within an architecture comprising a Management and Coordination Agent Hub, Specialized Execution Agents, and a Knowledge Support Layer. Additionally, the study develops an information extraction algorithm based on LLMs and structured prompts, as well as a hybrid risk identification algorithm combining rules and semantic models. The revision further clarifies cost-aware selective invocation, confidence-based conflict resolution, separate precision-recall evaluation, and a human-in-the-loop knowledge update workflow.
