AB  - Aiming at the problems of disconnection among creative generation, physiological adaptation and additive manufacturing, insufficient manufacturability constraints and low design automation in personalized wearable device design, this paper proposes a closed-loop collaborative design framework embedded with manufacturing constraints. The framework adopts a three-tier modular architecture: design semantic-to-visual mapping, physiological data-driven geometric constraint solving, and additive manufacturing-oriented performance optimization. By integrating 3D printing process priors into the generative stage and multi-objective topology optimization, the framework realizes end-to-end intelligent customization from text to printable models. Verified on a VR headset mask, the design cycle is shortened from days to minutes; the average fitting accuracy reaches 0.37 mm; the average printing yield reaches 98.5%; and the model weight is reduced by 25.2%. The method provides a reusable paradigm for the large-scale personalized production of wearable devices. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
