AB  - Traditional visual communication design has problems with long creation cycles and difficult style control, making it difficult to ssmeet the multi platform and high-frequency content production needs in digital communication scenarios. In response to the above challenges, we propose a style adaptive generative AI for digital visual communication called StyleGrad. This method uses MT5 as the basic model for extending prompt words, modeling style intensity as a gradient distribution in the semantic space, achieving automatic extension from short style words to multi-level detailed prompt words. We design Style Intensity Contrastive Loss (SIC Loss) to form an ordered semantic distribution of different intensity descriptions through triplet constraints, and introduce cross modal consistency loss constraints to generate semantic alignment between text and style images, ensuring that the expanded prompts faithfully preserve the core visual semantics of the target style. In addition, we constructed a style description dataset containing 50 visual styles across four categories and 5000 samples, each with standardized low, medium, and high intensity descriptions paired with reference images. The experimental results showed that StyleGrad achieved a generation quality of FID 23.76 and CLIP Score 0.318, with a style intensity control accuracy of 76.42%. Compared to the suboptimal method, it improved by 8.01%, 3.25%, and 3. 14% respectively, validating the effectiveness of the proposed approach in controllable style generation for digital visual communication.
