AB  - As a world cultural heritage, Dunhuang murals have suffered varying degrees of damage due to their age and environmental factors. Traditional manual restoration carries the risk of secondary damage, while digital restoration techniques, although avoiding physical contact, face challenges in addressing large-area damage and complex structural repairs with existing methods. In recent years, the emergence and breakthroughs in generative artificial intelligence (AI) technology have provided new solutions for the restoration of Dunhuang murals.This paper explores the key technologies of generative AI in mural restoration. By introducing a GAN model that incorporates local texture matching and global feature fusion, a hybrid restoration framework combining traditional texture synthesis and deep generative networks is proposed. Digital image inpainting involves automatically or semi-automatically repairing damaged or missing parts of an image through computer algorithms. This field has spawned various inpainting models, which can be categorized based on image information diffusion methods into diffusion-based models, texture synthesis-based models, and generative adversarial network (GAN)-based models, among others. This paper focuses on the application of generative AI in the restoration of Dunhuang murals, systematically reviews its core principles, evaluates restoration effects through case studies, and discusses future development directions, providing references for researchers and cultural heritage conservation work. © 2026 SPIE.
