TI  - Research on the Construction of Subjectivity in Art Education in the AI Era
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 1477
EP  - 1483
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821144
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821144
AB  - With the rapid popularization of generative artificial intelligence (AI), art creation has entered a human-computer symbiosis era featuring data-driven generation, algorithmic interaction, and intelligent collaboration. Art students, as core practitioners of AI-assisted creation, are confronted with the critical problem of how to construct and maintain creative subjectivity under human-AI collaboration. This study adopts a mixed research method combining questionnaire survey and in-depth interview, and uses statistical algorithms including independent samples t-test, one-way ANOVA, and Pearson correlation analysis to process 303 valid samples from an art university. The results show that AI usage presents two typical patterns: human-dominant creation and AI-dominant creation. Students with high subjectivity perceive stronger AI empowerment in efficiency improvement and technical iteration, while those with low subjectivity face risks such as reduced originality and increased cognitive offloading. Grade, major, and AI familiarity are significant influencing factors of subjectivity. This paper constructs a phased teaching framework for AI-integrated art education based on statistical inference and subjectivity protection, providing a quantitative reference for the digital transformation of art education.
