TI  - Streets as Shared Canvases: An In-Situ Mixed Reality Platform for Citizen-Led Urban Restorative Design
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia
VL  - 3
SP  - 103
EP  - 112
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048875594?origin=resultslist
AB  - Urban streets are vital public interfaces where spatial form and everyday movement converge to shape human well-being. As health-oriented urban design increasingly values restorativeness, current evaluation methods remain largely detached from lived street experience. Photographic audits and lab-based virtual walkthroughs capture static form but overlook embodied and dynamic dimensions. To address this gap, this study develops an In-Situ Mixed Reality (ISMR) platform that frames streets as shared canvases for citizen-led restorative design. The platform enables on-site mixed reality (MR) assessments of perceived restorativeness. The MR environment situates perception, interaction, and design through large language models and context-aware visual synthesis. Field deployment revealed that localized social, micro-spatial, and sensory affordances, such as resting amenities, spatial maintenance, and micro greenery, exert a strong influence on perceived restorativeness. These findings indicate that restorative potential emerges not from monumental spatial form in isolation but through the cooperative interplay between large-scale morphology and assorted human-scale affordances of street life. By foregrounding perception as design intelligence, the study suggests new pathways for embedded human-AI collaboration in shaping emotionally responsive and citizen-authored urban environments. © 2026 and published by the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA), Hong Kong.
