AB  - This study explores the IP evolution of AI-driven virtual actors across cross-media environments. By integrating multimodal generative models such as Stable Diffusion, Sora, and AudioCraft, it proposes a systematic AI Persona IP framework covering character creation, narrative expansion, and brand consolidation. Redundant descriptions of the framework are condensed, emphasizing its unique contribution to IP lifecycle extension. A comparative perspective is introduced by aligning the model with classical frameworks such as Keller's Brand Equity Model and Aaker's Brand Building Process, demonstrating its alignment with established IP management theory. Empirical results based on 1,200 multimodal samples and 300,000 interaction records show that AI virtual actors outperform human actors in lifecycle duration, communication reach, and ROI, with 33% higher fan retention and 60% more brand collaborations. The findings indicate that AI virtual humans are becoming key intelligent assets in the digital content economy, reshaping creative production and value-chain structures. This work provides theoretical insight and a practical framework for sustainable development within the intelligent entertainment ecosystem.
