AB  - This study originated from the author's field observation in Yixing purple clay teaware production area—traditional teaware workshops are facing the dilemma of lacking successors. To explore the feasible path of integrating artificial intelligence technology with the traditional “Dao-Qi-Yong” philosophy, the research team spent three months visiting Yixing, Jingdezhen, and Dehua, constructing a “form-semantics” correlation database containing over 5,000 natural image images (bamboo 24%, landscape 30%, cloud pattern 18%, etc.) and more than 80 cultural semantic keywords. On this basis, the CLIP cross-modal learning model (Top-5 accuracy 73.6%) and conditional generative adversarial network (FID reduced below 20) were introduced to achieve intention-driven generation from cultural semantics such as “bamboo+flowing water+quiet vitality” to three-dimensional forms. The design practice with “Zhuyun Zhiliu” intelligent teapot as a case study shows that this framework can effectively support teaware creation with both cultural connotation and intelligent function. User evaluation by 30 participants (8 design professionals, 12 tea culture enthusiasts, 10 ordinary consumers) showed that the design in this paper was significantly better than traditional design in aesthetic satisfaction (6.2±0.75), cultural identity (6.5±0.72), and user experience (6.0±0.81) (p<0.001), with GRA grey correlation degree reaching 0.89, verifying the effectiveness of the “Dao-Qi-Yong” ternary framework in the context of intelligent design.
