AB  - With the rapid advancement of generative artificial intelligence (gAI), spatial design is undergoing a paradigm shift from rule-based and procedural workflows toward intelligent, adaptive, and collaborative creation. This study proposes a systematic generative AI-driven design framework for exhibition spaces that integrates thematic modeling, multimodal prompt reasoning, and multi-objective optimization. The framework transforms curatorial intent and audience needs into structured inputs to generate feasible, semantically coherent, and human-aligned exhibition layouts. A hybrid optimization model is employed to balance accessibility, narrative consistency, immersion, cost, and energy efficiency, while an iterative human–AI co-design mechanism enhances adaptability and interpretability. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves superior performance over traditional and deep generative baselines in both design quality and convergence efficiency, improving mean objective performance by over 25%. Furthermore, user studies confirm enhanced designer trust and control through visual–semantic interpretability. Overall, this research establishes a comprehensive methodology for intelligent, explainable, and efficient exhibition space design, providing a theoretical and practical foundation for future applications of gAI in architectural and spatial design.
