AB  - This chapter examines the role of virtual reality (VR) and artificial intelligence (AI) in bereavement care, focusing on their ethical implications for palliative social work practice within multidisciplinary palliative care settings. It draws on the South Korean documentary series I Met You (Seasons 1–4, 2020–2024), which depicts bereaved people engaging in virtual encounters with deceased family members. Employing a philosophically informed virtual ethnography, the chapter proposes a five-dimensional ethical framework for practice: ‘emotional safety; relational ethics; ritual design; boundaries of representation; and memory grounded in structural injustice’. Framed through Michael Polanyi’s concept of tacit knowledge, it argues that while AI technologies can simulate presence and interaction, they cannot assume the capacity to bear responsibility for vulnerability, moral consequence, and relational accountability. The chapter positions palliative care practitioners as ‘human circuit breakers’ within AI-mediated bereavement care, with responsibility for safeguarding ethical boundaries and ensuring that technology functions as a bridge back to life rather than a mechanism for indefinite suspension in grief. The chapter concludes by calling for interdisciplinary safeguards against the ‘narrative violence’ of generative AI and for the protection of the dignity of the digitally resurrected, understood here as time-limited, representational reconstructions rather than enduring agents. © 2027 selection and editorial matter, Goetz Ottmann and Carolyn Noble; individual chapters, the contributors.
