AB  - Gesture coding in collaborative virtual reality (VR) environments is theoretically central to studying embodied collaborative problem-solving (CPS), yet scalable, theory-driven approaches remain underdeveloped. This paper reports preliminary work on VR Gesture Analyst, an AI-assisted multimodal gesture coding tool powered by Gemini 2.5. The tool’s core design principle draws on McNeill’s gesture-speech unity framework: rather than coding gestures from visual signals alone, the system jointly reasons over synchronized video and audio, enabling verbal context to inform gesture classification. We describe the tool’s architecture and an AI-assisted coding workflow. Preliminary results reveal two systematic error types: verbal over-reliance and VR-manipulating actions misclassification. These findings highlight both the potential and current limitations of AI-assisted multimodal gesture coding. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
